  "preamble": "Debuccalization of the aspirated stop [tʰ] to [h] removes the oral closure and keeps only the aspiration. It is a regional Sinitic development: in the Northern Min variety of Jianyang, Proto-Min initial *tʰ became h- (天 'sky', 鐵 'iron'), and in Taishanese and other Siyi Yue dialects the aspirated initial of Middle Chinese 透 words is h- where Cantonese keeps tʰ (天 Cantonese tin1, Taishanese hen1).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Debuccalization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Jianyang (Northern Min)",
      "languageFamily": "Min",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "天 'sky', Proto-Min *tʰ-",
          "to": "Jianyang h-",
          "note": "Xiamen, Fuzhou, Jian'ou and Shaowu keep tʰ (Proto-Min correspondence table)."
        },
        {
          "from": "鐵 'iron', Proto-Min *tʰ-",
          "to": "Jianyang h-",
          "note": "The same correspondence holds for 炭 'charcoal' and 腿 'leg'."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Taishanese",
      "languageFamily": "Yue",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "天 'sky', Middle Chinese tʰ- (Cantonese tin1)",
          "to": "hen1"
        },
        {
          "from": "偷 'to steal', Middle Chinese tʰ- (Cantonese tau1)",
          "to": "heu1 (hau33)"
        },
        {
          "from": "鐵 'iron', Middle Chinese tʰ- (Cantonese tit3)",
          "to": "het1",
          "note": "Also 湯 'soup' hong1, 他 'he' ha1, 太 'too' hai1; Cantonese aspirated stops regularly correspond to Siyi /h/."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Prakrit",
